  "course_description": "Selected poetry and prose works of nineteenth century American authors such as Emerson, Thoreau, Melville, Hawthorne, Whitman, Dickinson, Howells, James, and Twain.",
  "course_notes": "Nineteenth century U.S. culture shares an uncanny resemblance to modern day culture, energized as it was by abolitionists, disease epidemics, incels, feminism and its backlash, alongside anxieties about consumerism, migration, national disunity, and urbanization. In this course, we will survey major writers of nineteenth century America to explore how they innovated new literary genres and forms to confront these crises of national cohesion and identity. We will connect insights from these historical readings to present-day issues in U.S. culture. Readings will span prose and poetry, including works by Edgar Allan Poe, Harriet Jacobs, Walt Whitman, Emily Dickinson, Herman Melville, Sui Sin Far, Charles Chesnutt, and Henry James.",
